course: space
lesson: 2
title: How the Solar System was born
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: About 4.6 billion years ago there was no Sun and no Earth, only a huge, cold cloud of gas and dust called the solar nebula.
explain: Gravity pulled the cloud together, and as it shrank it spun faster and spread into a flat disc, just as pizza dough spreads out when a cook spins it.
explain: Most of the gas fell into the middle, which got so hot and squashed that fusion switched on: the Sun was born.
explain: In the disc, dust stuck into pebbles, pebbles into rocks, rocks into small worlds, rolling bigger like a snowball, and these crashed and merged into planets.
explain: Near the hot young Sun only rock and metal stayed solid, so the inner planets are small and rocky; further out ice could form too, and the outer planets grew into giants.
fact: The Solar System formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a huge cloud of gas and dust called the solar nebula, which collapsed under its own gravity.
fact: As the cloud collapsed it spun faster and flattened into a disc, which is why the planets orbit in nearly the same flat plane.
fact: All eight planets go round the Sun in the same direction, the same way the Sun itself spins.
fact: Most of the gas fell to the centre, and when it became hot and dense enough, fusion switched on and the Sun was born.
fact: In the disc, dust grains stuck together into pebbles, then rocks, then bodies kilometres wide called planetesimals, which crashed and merged into planets.
fact: Close to the Sun only rock and metal could stay solid, but beyond the frost line, around where the asteroid belt is now, ice could form too, so the outer planets grew much bigger.
fact: Jupiter and Saturn grew solid cores about ten times the Earth's mass, then quickly pulled in vast amounts of hydrogen and helium gas.
fact: Scientists think the Moon formed when a Mars-sized body, nicknamed Theia, smashed into the young Earth and threw out debris that gathered into the Moon; this is the leading idea, not proven beyond doubt.
fact: The giant planets probably moved their orbits after they formed, scattering many small icy bodies inward and outward.
fact: The oldest solid bits in meteorites, space rocks that fell to Earth, are about 4.57 billion years old, and their radioactive atoms, which slowly change at a steady rate like a clock, let scientists date the birth of the Solar System.
fact: Astronomers have found more than 6,000 planets around other stars.
fact: Telescopes such as ALMA in Chile have photographed dusty discs around young stars where new planets are forming right now.
exercise: What was the Solar System made from? | A huge cloud of gas and dust called the solar nebula | A piece that broke off another, much bigger star long before | Rocks thrown out by the Big Bang the year before | Water that froze in empty space
exercise: About how long ago did the Solar System form? | About 4.6 billion years ago | About 6,000 years ago | About 4.6 million years ago | About 46 billion years ago
exercise: What made the cloud collapse? | Its own gravity | The wind from the Sun | Heat from the planets | A magnet at its centre
exercise: Why do the planets orbit in nearly the same flat plane? | The collapsing cloud spun faster and flattened into a disc | The Sun's light pushes them all into one straight line, like a wind | They were placed there one by one later | They are held flat by a giant ring of ice
exercise: Which way do the planets go round the Sun? | All the same way, the way the Sun spins | Half one way round and half the other way | Each one in its own random direction round the Sun | The inner ones one way, the giants the other
exercise: How was the Sun born? | Gas fell to the centre until it was hot and dense enough for fusion | A planet caught fire and grew bigger | Lightning struck the centre of the cloud | Two planets crashed together and burst into flame, lighting up the cloud
exercise: What are planetesimals? | Bodies kilometres wide built from dust that merged into planets | Tiny planets that orbit other moons instead of going round the Sun | Pieces broken off the Sun | Comets that have lost their tails
exercise: What is the frost line? | The distance from the Sun beyond which ice could form | The edge of the Earth's polar ice | The line where the Sun's light stops | The border between two planets' orbits, where neither can pass
exercise: Why are the outer planets so much bigger than the inner ones? | Beyond the frost line ice was solid too, so there was more to build with | They were further from the Sun's gravity so they could stretch | They are made of lighter stuff that puffed up | The inner planets were later eaten away by the Sun
exercise: Why did Jupiter and Saturn end up mostly made of gas? | Their heavy cores pulled in vast amounts of hydrogen and helium | They were born from a bubble of gas inside the Sun | Their rock melted and turned into gas | They swallowed the gas from the inner planets' oceans and their skies
exercise: How do scientists think the Moon formed? | A Mars-sized body, Theia, hit the young Earth and the debris gathered | The Earth caught a passing moon from far away | The Moon broke off the Sun | The Moon was made from frozen ocean water
exercise: What did the giant planets probably do after they formed? | Moved their orbits, scattering small icy bodies | Stayed fixed exactly where they began, and never moved | Crashed into each other and joined into one giant | Fell into the Sun and then came back out of it again
exercise: How do scientists know the age of the Solar System? | By the radioactive atoms in the oldest bits of meteorites | By counting the layers of rock, like the rings inside the Earth | By measuring how hot the Sun is and how fast it is cooling | By reading the old star maps drawn by the ancient astronomers
exercise: About how many planets around other stars have been found? | More than 6,000 | About 9 | Exactly 100 so far | None yet
exercise: What have telescopes photographed around young stars? | Dusty discs where planets are forming now | Finished Solar Systems exactly like ours | Giant rings of pure water | Nothing, because other stars have no planets
test: my sister says the planets were all made at different times from bits of other stars. what's closer to the truth? | They all formed together from the same spinning disc of gas and dust | Each planet came from a different star | The Earth came first and the others millions of years later | The planets were made after the Sun burnt out
test: Spin a ball of pizza dough and it spreads out flat. What in the Solar System's story is like that? | The spinning cloud flattening into a disc | The Sun slowly cooling down after it switched on | The planets crashing together into bigger ones | The Moon being knocked off the Earth by a crash
test: A bicycle pump gets warm when you squeeze air into it. What does this help explain? | How squeezing gas in the cloud's centre made it hot enough to start the Sun | Why the planets spin | Why Jupiter is striped | Why the Moon has craters
test: If the young Earth had formed out beyond the frost line, what might have happened? | It could have grabbed ice as well as rock and grown much bigger | It would have been made only of metal | It would have become a star | Nothing, every planet ends up the same
test: Which evidence from meteorites helps date the Solar System? | Their oldest solid bits contain radioactive atoms that act like a clock | They are always still warm when they land | They carry written messages from the past | They have tree rings inside them
test: A game starts as an empty map of fog that slowly fills in with land. What part of the real story is like that? | Dust and gas slowly clumping into planets | The Sun suddenly exploding into a bright flash | The planets being made all at once, in one day | Earth being carved out of the Moon by a giant
test: Rolling a snowball makes it pick up more snow the bigger it gets. Which stage is like that? | Pebbles and rocks sticking together into planetesimals | The Sun switching on | The planets moving their orbits slowly further out over time | Light leaving the Sun
test: Jake: "The Moon is just a captured asteroid, everyone knows that." What do most scientists actually think? | It probably formed from debris after Theia hit the Earth | He is right, it is a captured asteroid | It was a planet that drifted in from another star a long time ago | It was made by the Sun splitting in half
test: Why do the small icy bodies far out in the Solar System matter to this story? | The moving giant planets probably scattered them there | They are where the Sun keeps its spare fuel for later on | They are the remains of a planet that exploded | They were placed there after the Moon formed
test: What do discs around young stars tell us about our own Solar System? | They show the same kind of birth that our Solar System probably had | They prove our Solar System is unique | They show that planets form from stars dying | They tell us nothing about planets
test: why are the planets all going the same way round, did someone line em up or what | They inherited the spin of the cloud they formed from | Yes, a passing star lined them up | They chase each other round and round, pulled by magnetism | They are pulled along by the Moon
exam: Identify the material from which the Solar System is understood to have formed. | A collapsing cloud of gas and dust designated the solar nebula | The debris of a planet destroyed by the Sun | A great quantity of water ejected by a distant star into empty space | Fragments of the Moon dispersed by an impact
exam: State the principal reason for which the outer planets attained a considerably greater size than the inner planets. | Beyond the frost line ices were able to condense in addition to rock | The outer planets were subject to a weaker solar wind | The outer planets were formed considerably earlier in time than the inner | The outer planets consumed the inner planets' atmospheres
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the prevailing account of the origin of the Moon? | Debris from a collision of a Mars-sized body with the Earth coalesced | The Moon was captured intact from the asteroid belt | The Moon was detached from the Sun in its infancy | The Moon was formed subsequent to the appearance of life upon the Earth
exam: Ascertain the means by which the age of the Solar System has been determined. | Radioactive dating of the oldest solid components of meteorites | Measurement of the present temperature of the Sun | Enumeration of the craters upon the Earth's surface and the Moon's | Calculation from the number of known planets
